ut more people in body bags over  the last two years than i have in the  last nine  it's hard  and there's just a heaviness  [Music]  so  that  um in an effort to preserve my own  mental emotional health was a uh  helped with the decision to  do something  a little different  the exhausting and polarized climate has  also led to troubling protest signs like  this one outside the er calling doctors  murderers  i feel for all of us who are trying to  fight  fight against covet and provide care and  that's what the greeting is  now more than 18 months into a pandemic  that once celebrated these workers as  heroes dr megan mcinerney says today she  thinks twice about wearing her scrubs in  public  is it because i feel unsafe i don't  necessarily think so but i'm certainly  not going to put myself out there i'm  not going to you know put up the sign  that i'm a health care worker  once celebrated as heroes today some at  times feel like the enemy one rn in this  icu actually left to become a checkout  clerk at the local walmart she said the  stress the long hours and the  frustration simply became too much gail  boy jonathan it's a heartbreaking story  because all the medical people want to  do is save our lives and i'm wondering  how they're coping and what they tell  themselves to keep going when it seems  their work may not be appreciated  because of politics makes no sense to me  yeah it makes no sense to me too these  are incredibly strong people a few  minutes into each of our interviews all  of them at points started to cry the  hope that they do have comes from the  lives that they do save there are so  many people in the overflowing covet  wards here when one person gets out they  jump around here in this icu to  celebrate those small victories to get  through it all  yeah they should please know a lot of  people appreciate the work they do and  we need them desperately jonathan we  thank you
 

